WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:08.000
David, is there an actual scientific consensus&nbsp;
on what covid policy should be? This is a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.160
great question, because there are people who&nbsp;
hear the question, can't we just empirically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:20.080
determine the right covid policy? And the&nbsp;
problem with the question is that it ignores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.280
that every choice is subject to trade&nbsp;
offs. So I'll give you an example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:34.240
It has been determined beyond a doubt that given.&nbsp;
That the contagion is mostly happening indoors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:41.520
if you limit indoor exposure without masks to&nbsp;
other people, you dramatically reduce your risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:48.800
of giving to giving or getting the virus from&nbsp;
someone. Most contagion is happening within homes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.480
and it's being brought in from elsewhere. And&nbsp;
of course, you reduce the risk of bringing it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.520
from elsewhere. If when you go to work, if you're&nbsp;
working indoors or go to the grocery store or you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:06.960
wear a mask. OK, so it can be easy to say that's&nbsp;
the science. But when you talk about policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.440
you're talking about a tradeoff. There are people&nbsp;
I disagree with them, but there are people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.640
I walked by a guy on the street the other day&nbsp;
who chastised me for wearing a mask in a bakery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.320
as required by the bakery, who say, well, OK, that&nbsp;
may be the science of masks, but I don't believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.160
the trade off is worth it. The trade off being I&nbsp;
don't like the way I look and it's uncomfortable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.560
That's a trade off. And the science can't tell&nbsp;
you if that trade off is worth it because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.920
relates to values. Fundamentally, the the&nbsp;
bigger one, you know, that that would apply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:45.440
is temporary shutdowns during spikes slow the&nbsp;
transmission when they are adhered to. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.760
there's a very interesting article I recently read&nbsp;
about. You know, a lot of Latin American countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.480
on paper have the strictest shutdowns and yet the&nbsp;
virus is out of control. What's happening? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.680
people aren't abiding by the shutdown and&nbsp;
there's no enforcement of the shutdown as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:04.880
often as is the case. So even if you determine&nbsp;
temporary shutdowns that are very finely tuned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.520
are clinically effective and they also minimize&nbsp;
societal harm in terms of economic harm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.760
and you're feeling like your personal liberties&nbsp;
have been taken away. These are like targeted, OK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.680
it's still a trade off, right? You still have to&nbsp;
say, well, this is the this scientific policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.240
This policy has been shown scientifically to&nbsp;
reduce transmission by this amount. It lowers the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:34.560
are not from something to something lower&nbsp;
than that. You still have a counter to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.360
and you still have to decide based on your&nbsp;
values, whether it's worth the trade off. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.840
I think that when we're asking the question,&nbsp;
what is the scientific consensus on covid policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.560
what you're really saying is how much do different&nbsp;
policies reduce transmission? That the societal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.920
question, the government question, the cultural&nbsp;
question, the economic question is what is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:04.000
economic cost? What is the psychological cost,&nbsp;
depression or separation of people, loneliness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.440
et cetera, economic, all this other stuff. And&nbsp;
that is a different question than simply what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:15.360
works scientifically. And to me, I continue to&nbsp;
be shocked that people are insisting on this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.080
We we can't do all of this because we have to&nbsp;
save the economy. We can't shut down this. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:25.040
can't do that. We give all this all this different&nbsp;
stuff. How are people still thinking that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.840
economy versus health, if we seriously&nbsp;
had dealt with the virus at the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:38.960
there might have been an initial economic hit that&nbsp;
would have been harder. But we're a year in. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.600
on Spike three. While most European countries&nbsp;
are only on the second spike, we've got one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.680
the highest death rates in the world. If you look&nbsp;
at the deaths relative to our population, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:54.400
completely off the charts. So the the idea that&nbsp;
we're also dealing with false dichotomies about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.280
it's either health or the economy, the economy&nbsp;
has suffered greatly and will continue to suffer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:06.560
And we're doing disastrously poorly when it when&nbsp;
it comes to health. So we might imagine that all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.600
that matters is understanding the scientific&nbsp;
consensus like targeted and accu accurate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:17.680
really well done. Lockdown's work to&nbsp;
prevent the spread if people really do it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:24.880
But there are downsides. Depression, maybe people&nbsp;
drinking a business, permanently shutting down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:31.200
economic problems, all that stuff. But there&nbsp;
is no consensus about what is worth it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.040
that's where the conflict is coming from.&nbsp;
Now some people are making legal arguments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:41.680
A government, a state government mask mandate&nbsp;
is unconstitutional. No, it's not. But there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.840
real questions about what we believe is worth it,&nbsp;
and we came out on the wrong side of it, lastly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.120
that that's the important thing to know,&nbsp;
which is if we had actually had consensus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.200
on, you know, a two month, we're 10&nbsp;
months in, we're almost a year. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.960
we had done a proper two month shutdown and really&nbsp;
done everything that that should have been done.&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.280
We could have had a relatively normal&nbsp;
summer in many parts of the of the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.920
as many European countries did.&nbsp;
That's that's the unfortunate reality.&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:13.600
And getting consensus, we can better get&nbsp;
consensus on the scientific, even though you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.320
still have people who say masks don't actually&nbsp;
work, shutdowns don't actually work. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.120
if people don't wear the masks correctly or if you&nbsp;
announce a shutdown but don't adhere to it yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:28.880
that won't work. But if you do it right, it does.&nbsp;
Where we have not been able to get consensus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.280
is what is worth doing and what are people&nbsp;
willing to tolerate. And part of it goes back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:38.080
to the complete bungling by the federal government&nbsp;
at the very start of this entire thing. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.560
got a great bonus show for you today.
